/* torrentxt.map - the linker version script for the shipped library.
 *
 * WHY, and why the comment this replaces was wrong. torrentxt/CMakeLists.txt
 * used to carry an EMPTY link-options block whose comment said a version
 * script "is not needed because the explicit BTX_API attribute already gates
 * the surface". That is true of OUR translation units and false of the shipped
 * artifact: -fvisibility=hidden and BTX_API reach the code this project
 * compiles, and libtorrent, Boost and the statically linked OpenSSL are built
 * by their own targets, out of reach of our flags. A static archive's symbols
 * inherit the shared library's default (public) visibility, so they land in
 * the dynamic export table regardless.
 *
 * MEASURED, not assumed: tools/check-binary-freshness.py records that the
 * committed Linux builds "export 5419 statically-linked libtorrent/boost/
 * libstdc++ symbols beyond the shim's own" - which is why that gate SKIPS
 * torrentxt's export-closure leg entirely. The claim and the measurement had
 * been sitting one gate apart for months.
 *
 * It became load-bearing on 2026-08-26. The universal mac dylib is built by
 * lipo-ing a per-architecture build, and each architecture's OpenSSL brings
 * its OWN dispatch symbols - ChaCha20_ctr32_sve and OPENSSL_arm_midr on
 * arm64, OPENSSL_ia32_cpuid and OPENSSL_ia32_rdrand_bytes on x86_64. With
 * nothing filtering the export table the two slices therefore ship DIFFERENT
 * export sets, and check-binary-freshness refuses a fat library whose slices
 * disagree, because no per-set verdict is honest about one. Filtering at link
 * time makes both slices export the same thing: the ABI.
 *
 * btx::test::* is exported ON PURPOSE, exactly as datachannelxt exports
 * dcx::test::*: tests/torrent_smoke_test.cpp links the REAL shared library and
 * calls btx::test::live_session_count() to prove the session actually died.
 * A test hook that the shipped artifact hides cannot check the shipped
 * artifact.
 *
 * -fvisibility=hidden stays. This is the belt to its braces, and it is the
 * only one of the two that reaches a dependency's symbols.
 *
 * WHERE IT MUST NOT BE APPLIED (added 2026-08-26, hours after the above, by
 * the CI run that landed it). `local: *` hides more than the dependencies: this
 * library links -static-libstdc++ -static-libgcc, and -fvisibility=hidden does
 * not reach an archive's own objects, so WITHOUT this script the .so also
 * exports a full copy of the C++ runtime - measured on the CI toolchain, 781
 * symbols including 16 __cxa_* and operator new/delete, cut to 1 by this file.
 * Whether that copy is private or shared is therefore this file's decision.
 *
 * Private is right for the shipped artifact (everything static inside it, and
 * the OXT engine that loads it is C - no other C++ runtime in the process) and
 * wrong when libtorrent comes from the system as a SHARED library, because
 * libtorrent-rasterbar.so then brings libstdc++.so.6 and exceptions thrown
 * inside libtorrent would be caught by a firewall running on a different
 * __cxa_* implementation. torrent_smoke_test aborted with no output at all in
 * exactly that lane while the same commit passed ctest on the two Linux lanes
 * that build libtorrent from source. torrentxt/CMakeLists.txt therefore skips
 * this script when TORRENTXT_USE_SYSTEM_LIBTORRENT is on, and says so.
 */
{
    global:
        btx_*;
        _ZN3btx4test*;   /* btx::test::* - see above */
    local:
        *;
};
